  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Storm chances linger through at least Saturday,\nwith a possibly drier finish to the weekend.\n\nMost of the heavy shower activity of the DC metro earlier this\nmorning has pushed east and out of the area. Additional light to\nmoderate showers are noted over northern MD, pushing east to the\nBaltimore area. A few light showers possible elsewhere, though\nit looks to be mostly dry into the afternoon. \n\nAdditional showers and a few thunderstorms develop this afternoon\nto evening over the Potomac Highlands into the central Virginia\nPiedmont region. Overall conditions today will yield more \nclouds than sun. This is accompanied by high temperatures in the\nmid 70s to low 80s, locally a bit cooler across the mountains. \n\nThe cold front initially north of the Mason-Dixon Line is\nforecast to settle toward the Virginia-North Carolina border by\ntonight. Despite the frontal passage, its more diffuse nature\nwill only bring a limited drop in temperatures and humidity\nlevels. Saturday morning will start off in the 60s which comes\nwith further shower and thunderstorm chances throughout the day.\nThe focus is driven by an elongated trough currently crossing\nthe Arklatex states. As this array of embedded shortwaves lift\nacross the Tennessee Valley toward the southern Mid-Atlantic,\nstorm chances increase in earnest. The 00Z high-resolution model\nsuite shows an uptick in rainfall amounts, some of which are on\nthe higher end (1 to 2 inches in 24 hours). With precipitable\nwater values in the 1.75 to 2.00 inch range and deep warm cloud\nlayers, some fairly efficient storms are possible in this\nenvironment. As such, the Weather Prediction Center supports a\nSlight risk for excessive rainfall on Saturday, especially\nacross the I-95 urban corridor. Will continue to monitor the\nsituation as new mesoscale guidance arrives.\n\nComing off temperatures similar to today, expect a slight warm\nup into Sunday with highs returning to the mid 80s. Some\nresidual shower activity is possible to start the day. However,\nthe local area will be in the wake of the passing shortwave\ntrough. Expect some increase in humidity as the boundary to the\nsouth returns northward as a warm front. Unlike previous days, a\nfair amount of sunshine is possible, particularly for the second\nhalf of the day. Sunday night's low temperatures will fall into\nthe 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm ahead of a mid-week cold front.\n\n500mb ridge builds Mon through Wed, before weakening as SW\ntroughing and associated sfc cold front move through midweek. \nSome spread on exactly what day this happens, but overall \nconsensus around Thu. This could bring showers and \nthunderstorms, but probability of svr wx remains low.",
